From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 20:39:48 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: um: implement non-streaming SVE task state for Darwin
 guests

Bridge ABI 21 lets the Darwin host implement non-streaming SVE for a
run. When it does, darwin_uml_guest_sve_vector_length() reports its
vector length, 16 bytes on every host, and the kernel gives each task
what arch/arm64/kernel/fpsimd.c gives it on a CPU whose only SVE vector
length is 16 bytes. When it reports zero, every new path is inactive and
the kernel behaves as before.

Every arm64 bridge tail grows from 552 to 592 bytes: after the SME
registers come P0-P15 and FFR, 16 bits each, a live flag and the run's
vector length. At this length each Z register is its V register, so P
and FFR are all the state SVE adds, and the packed predicates are
already in their signal-frame and NT_ARM_SVE form. The live flag is the
image of native TIF_SVE: the host sets it when the task executes SVE
outside streaming mode, and the kernel clears it where native Linux
stops tracking the state.

The kernel duties follow native arm64:

- A system call discards P and FFR and leaves the task not live, before
  a tracer's syscall-entry stop, as fpsimd_syscall_enter() does when
  the state is saved during the call.
- Signal frames carry sve_context with the vector length, and the
  registers while the task is live. sigreturn makes the task live with
  the frame's P and FFR, or not live for a bare header, and rejects
  another vector length; V still comes from fpsimd_context. Without
  SME, frames take the extended layout too and carry no SME record.
- ptrace gains NT_ARM_SVE, in a view of its own without SME and before
  the SME regsets with it; it gives only a header in streaming mode.
- PR_SVE_SET_VL and PR_SVE_GET_VL and abi.sve_default_vector_length
  accept and report the one length; AT_MINSIGSTKSZ counts the SVE data.
- fork and exec leave the child or new image not live, and leaving
  streaming mode on the task's behalf discards the state, as native
  task_smstop_sm() leaves it in FPSIMD form.

The x86_64-userspace slice, which turns off UML_ARM64_SME, builds none
of it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
